/**
 * Finds the optimal wrap position within a given text segment, respecting a maximum line width.
 * This method is designed to identify a suitable index in the {@code text} where a line break
 * should occur, starting from {@code startPos} and attempting to keep the line length within {@code width}.
 *
 * The method prioritizes line breaks as follows:
 * 1. Explicit newline (`\n`) or tab (`\t`) characters appearing within the specified `width`.
 *    If found, the position immediately following that character is returned.
 * 2. The last whitespace character (excluding newline and tab) before or at the `startPos + width`
 *    boundary. If found, this position is returned.
 * 3. If no suitable whitespace break is found within the `width`, the method will break the word
 *    at the `startPos + width` boundary and return the position where that word ends. This handles
 *    cases where a single word exceeds the specified width.
 *
 * If the remaining text from `startPos` fits entirely within `width` (i.e., no wrapping is needed
 * for the current segment), or if a long word extends to the very end of the text, -1 is returned.
 *
 * @param text The input string to search for a wrap position.
 * @param width The maximum desired length for the current line segment, starting from {@code startPos}.
 * @param startPos The starting index in {@code text} from which to begin searching for a wrap position.
 *                 This index is inclusive.
 * @return The index in {@code text} where the line should be wrapped (exclusive of the character at this index
 *         for whitespace breaks, inclusive for newline/tab breaks indicating the start of the next segment),
 *         or -1 if no wrap is needed for the current segment (either because it fits or a long word extends to the end).
 * @throws IllegalArgumentException If {@code text} is null, {@code width} is not positive,
 *                                  or {@code startPos} is out of bounds for {@code text}.
 */
