/**
 * Converts a {@code String} into a {@code Number} object, attempting to
 * parse it as the most appropriate numerical type. This method supports
 * decimal, hexadecimal (with "0x" or "-0x" prefix), and scientific
 * notation. It also handles type suffixes 'l'/'L' for Long, 'f'/'F' for
 * Float, and 'd'/'D' for Double.
 *
 * <p>The parsing order for unsuffixed numbers (no 'l', 'f', 'd' suffix) is:
 * <ul>
 *     <li>If it appears to be an integer (no decimal point or exponent): {@link Integer}, then {@link Long}, then {@link BigInteger}.</li>
 *     <li>If it appears to be a floating-point number (has a decimal point or exponent): {@link Float}, then {@link Double}, then {@link BigDecimal}.</li>
 * </ul>
 *
 * <p>For suffixed numbers:
 * <ul>
 *     <li>'l' or 'L' suffix: Attempts to parse as {@link Long}, falling back to {@link BigInteger} if the value exceeds {@code Long.MAX_VALUE}.</li>
 *     <li>'f' or 'F' suffix: Attempts to parse as {@link Float}. If the value is infinite or underflows to zero from a non-zero string (indicating precision loss), it then attempts to parse as {@link Double} and finally {@link BigDecimal}.</li>
 *     <li>'d' or 'D' suffix: Attempts to parse as {@link Double}. If the value is infinite or underflows to zero from a non-zero string (indicating precision loss), it then attempts to parse as {@link BigDecimal}.</li>
 * </ul>
 *
 * <p>Hexadecimal strings (starting with "0x" or "-0x") are parsed as {@link Integer}, then {@link Long}, then {@link BigInteger}.
 *
 * @param str the {@code String} to convert. Can be {@code null}.
 * @return a {@code Number} object representing the string, or {@code null} if the input string is {@code null}.
 * @throws NumberFormatException if the string is blank, contains invalid characters, has an invalid format (e.g., exponent in wrong position), or an unsupported type suffix.
 *                               This exception is also thrown if a suffixed number cannot be parsed even by its fallback types (e.g., "123.abcF").
 */
